    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-250865",
      "knowledge_point_id": "07a3dfec-3f7b-5820-9be5-12bf4acd5a3b",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the First Term of an Arithmetic Series Given a Term and the Sum",
      "problem": "If the $10$th term of an arithmetic series is $-\\frac{1}{5}$ and the sum of the first $21$ terms is $0,$ then the first term of the series is ${a}_{1}=${{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "We are given one term of the series. So, to find the first term, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series in the form\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nSubstituting $N=21$ and ${S}_{N}=0$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{21}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(21-1)d) \\\\ 0 & =21(2{a}_{1}+20d) \\\\ 0 & =2{a}_{1}+20d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we can write an equation for the $10$th term of the arithmetic series and substitute in its known value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\ {a}_{10} & ={a}_{1}+(10-1)d \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & ={a}_{1}+9d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{5} & \\,\\,\\,=\\,{a}_{1}+9d \\\\ \\,0 & \\,\\,\\,=\\,2{a}_{1}+20d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we multiply the first equation by $-2$ and add the result to the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} & =-2{a}_{1}-18d \\\\ 0 & =2{a}_{1}+20d \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & =2d \\\\ d & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $d=\\frac{1}{5}$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{5} & ={a}_{1}+9d \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & ={a}_{1}+9(\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & ={a}_{1}+\\frac{9}{5} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
